    (v_topic_id, '248: What is the classic chest X-ray finding in a patient with Tetralogy of Fallot?', '["Egg on a string", "Figure 3 sign", "Boot-shaped heart (Coeur en sabot)", "Snowman sign"]', 2, 'The boot-shaped heart is due to right ventricular hypertrophy (upturned apex) and a concave main pulmonary artery segment. (Case Files Pediatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, '249: In Tetralogy of Fallot, the degree of cyanosis and clinical severity is most primarily determined by the severity of:', '["The amount of right ventricular hypertrophy", "The degree of aortic override", "Right ventricular outflow tract obstruction (Pulmonary stenosis)", "The size of the VSD"]', 2, 'The severity of pulmonary stenosis dictates the amount of right-to-left shunting across the VSD, which determines the degree of cyanosis. (Blueprints)'),
    (v_topic_id, '250: A 6-month-old with Tetralogy of Fallot presents with an acute hypercyanotic spell (Tet spell). After placing the child in a knee-chest position, which medication is most appropriate to administer?', '["Morphine and Oxygen", "Epinephrine", "Diuretics", "Digoxin"]', 0, 'Management of a Tet spell includes knee-chest position, oxygen, morphine (decreases sympathetic drive and infundibular spasm), and IV fluids. Beta-blockers or phenylephrine can also be used. (Nelson Essentials)'),
    (v_topic_id, '251: A neonate presents with profound cyanosis immediately after birth. Echocardiogram reveals that the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle. What is the diagnosis?', '["Transposition of the Great Arteries (TGA)", "Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Return", "Tetralogy of Fallot", "Truncus Arteriosus"]', 0, 'TGA is the most common cyanotic heart defect presenting in the immediate newborn period. It creates two parallel circulatory systems. (Nelson Essentials)'),
    (v_topic_id, '252: In a newborn with Transposition of the Great Arteries, survival before surgical repair depends entirely on:', '["Mixing of blood via a PDA, ASD, or VSD", "Keeping the infant in a knee-chest position", "Administration of 100% oxygen", "Administering Indomethacin"]', 0, 'Because the pulmonary and systemic circulations are in parallel, life is impossible without mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood through a shunt (PDA, PFO/ASD, or VSD). (Blueprints)'),
    (v_topic_id, '253: What is the classic chest X-ray appearance in a neonate with Transposition of the Great Arteries?', '["''Egg on a string'' with a narrow mediastinum", "Wall-to-wall heart", "Boot-shaped heart", "Snowman sign"]', 0, 'The ''egg on a string'' appearance is due to the anteroposterior relationship of the great vessels (causing a narrow superior mediastinum) and a globular heart. (BRS Pediatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, '254: If Prostaglandin E1 fails to improve oxygenation in a severely cyanotic neonate with Transposition of the Great Arteries, what is the next best step?', '["Immediate open-heart surgery", "High-frequency oscillatory ventilation", "Balloon atrial septostomy (Rashkind procedure)", "Administration of Indomethacin"]', 2, 'If the PFO is restrictive and PGE1 isn''t enough, a balloon atrial septostomy is performed via catheterization to tear the atrial septum and improve mixing. (Case Files Pediatrics)'),
